EXT. PACK-ICE FLOE - ARCTIC OCEAN - DAWN

A vast white plain, riven with blue cracks. A sled lies overturned, its runners splintered. Gray light bleeds into a pale horizon. The wind has dropped, leaving an unnatural hush.

Four dogs lie curled in the lee of the wreck. Beside them, five survivors huddle around a small spirit stove, its flame hardly bigger than a thumbnail.

CAPTAIN ISOLDE MARCHETTI-OKORO (50s, frost-crusted beard of scarf, steady eyes) crouches closest to the flame. DR. WILFRED ACHTERBERG (60s, bruised cheek, wire spectacles) rubs a bandaged hand. The navigator, TEMUJIN "TEMI" BOLDBAATAR (30s, parka hood rimmed with fur), studies a brass compass. The cook, GWENLLIAN PRYCE-ADEYEMI (40s, wool cap pulled to her eyebrows), stirs a pot of gray broth. And the youngest, CASPIAN "CAS" WOLFE (teens, voice cracked, eyelashes iced), sits wrapped in two blankets and shivers.

                    ISOLDE
          Rations.

                    GWENLLIAN
          Three days if we stretch it, Captain.

                    ISOLDE
          Stretch it.

                    WILFRED
          Isolde, the ice is moving.

                    ISOLDE
          I know, Wilfred.

                    TEMI
          Captain, the compass is spinning.

Isolde crosses to him and takes the compass. The needle swings in slow circles, never settling.

                    ISOLDE
          Close to the pole?

                    TEMI
          Too close. Or too far.

                    CAS
          Does that mean we're lost?

                    GWENLLIAN
          Hush, Cas. Drink your broth.

She hands him a tin cup. He takes it in both mittens.

                    ISOLDE
          Wilfred, the hand.

                    WILFRED
          It will mend.

                    ISOLDE
          It will blacken.

                    WILFRED
          Then it will blacken.

He tucks it inside his coat. Isolde regards him for a long moment, then turns away.

                    ISOLDE
          Temi, pick a heading. Any heading. We cannot stay.

                    TEMI
          East.

                    ISOLDE
          Why east?

                    TEMI
          Because the sun comes from there.

                    ISOLDE
          (almost smiling)
          That's as good a reason as any.

Cas lifts his head. His voice is small.

                    CAS
          Captain, will the dogs come?

                    ISOLDE
          The dogs always come.

                    GWENLLIAN
          Not Bramble. Bramble's leg.

                    ISOLDE
          Then we carry Bramble.

They all look at the old gray husky in the snow. Bramble thumps her tail once.

                    WILFRED
          Isolde, we can barely carry ourselves.

                    ISOLDE
          We will carry her anyway.

A silence settles. The stove flickers. Temi folds the compass shut and tucks it into his sleeve.

                    TEMI
          East, then.

                    GWENLLIAN
          East.

                    CAS
          East.

They begin to rise, one by one, creaking, stiff. Isolde kicks snow over the stove, and the flame dies. Wilfred lifts Bramble gently into his arms. Gwenllian shoulders the pack. Cas pulls his blankets tight. Temi checks the sky.

Isolde stands last, looking back at the broken sled. She lays a gloved hand on the splintered runner, as if on the flank of an animal.

                    ISOLDE
          (softly)
          Good work, old girl.

She turns and walks east, and the others fall in behind her, five dark figures dwindling on the endless white.
